1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:06,047
On the night of 21 August 1986, a lake in the hills of Cameroon exhaled.

2
00:00:06,597 --> 00:00:09,605
There was no fire. No flood. No explosion.

3
00:00:10,055 --> 00:00:14,644
Just a silent cloud of carbon dioxide, rising from the bottom of Lake Nyos.

4
00:00:15,094 --> 00:00:16,859
The gas was heavier than air.

5
00:00:17,209 --> 00:00:21,183
It flowed down the valleys, into the villages, while people slept.

6
00:00:21,783 --> 00:00:28,296
By morning, 1,746 people were dead, and 3,500 animals.

7
00:00:28,896 --> 00:00:33,793
Volcanic gas had been slowly building up deep in the water, until the lake could not hold it.

8
00:00:34,243 --> 00:00:38,374
Today, pipes vent that gas safely, shooting fountains high above the lake.

9
00:00:38,824 --> 00:00:42,627
And just two years earlier, nearby Lake Monoun had done the same.

10
00:00:43,177 --> 00:00:48,798
Would you live beside a lake that can breathe? And which deadly place on Earth should we explore next?
